[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is the last video we\'re going to release before shipping the Dart Solar to you guys. This has been four years in the making since 2022 till today. We\'ve had a ton of product improvements and lots of crazy adventures, but it has all'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'led to today. And here we are. And this is the Dart Solar, an expandable solar system that\'s about 15 ft long and collapses into about 5 ft. You can open and close it in about 10 seconds. It\'s'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only 1 in and a/4 tall when it breaks the wind. It\'s covered by a very strong polycarbonate shell that allows the air to pass through. The great thing about the Dart Solar is it connects with any'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'roof rack system to any vehicle, electric or not. So, we\'re going to cover how it connects to the roof rack of your vehicle and most importantly, how it connects to the electronics so that you can charge any vehicle with the'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Dart Solar. After we finish dissecting the Dart Solar, we\'re presenting our new Los Angeles factory near Korea Town where by the time you\'re watching this video, your Dart Solar is being assembled. So, as you can see from'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'above, one single Dart Solar unit is this side. You can see on this vehicle we have two dart solar units. You can tell because you can look at where the'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'polycarbonate shell ends. That\'s the end of the first module and then the second module. These are like Lego pieces. You can connect dart solar units together to create a 500 or a,000 watt system. Or if'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you have the roof area, you could make 1,500 2,000 watts. They connect based on the surface area that you have. It has three solar panels. One in the front'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that expands, one in the rear that expands, and one in the center that\'s always pointing to the sun. The great thing about always having a solar panel facing the sun in the middle is that as you\'re driving, you\'re still charging'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your power unit. The center solar panel charges with about 170 watts for each dart solar. So here I have two. So, when I\'m driving, at all times, if I\'m pointing to the sun, I\'m charging with'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '340 watts of solar. Another feature of the Dart solar that we\'re very excited about is that it can not only be installed in this orientation so that the solar panels expand to the front and'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the rear of the vehicle, but the Dart solar can also be installed side to side so that the solar panels expand like the doors of a vehicle. This allows for the solar panels to add shade or a type of'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cantal lever. So, if you have a pickup truck, you can install the dart solar to cover the truck bed as a cover or to provide extra power. You can also put it on top of the cabin of a pickup truck so'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that it expands laterally or the way that we have it installed here. Now, we\'re going to cover how we do that. How does it connect to the roof rack of a vehicle? Now, every Dart solar has this'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'super thick, heavy spine of a frame in the middle of it. And as you can see, this spine has two T- slots, one on either side. Each hole is for each'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'telescopic arm for each solar panel. We call it the double barrel spine. But in the T- slots, these almost halfin thick brackets go in. And as you can see, they'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can very easily slide into any direction, any length of the of the T- slots. Into the almost half an inch thick brackets goes a 3/8 in thick bolt that is then connected to another very'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thick solid aluminum plate. Now, everything under the dart solar that connects to your roof rack, we have it in black so that under the dart solar, it looks very uh sexy. This spine, as'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you could imagine, goes into the middle of the dart solar. So, we have four brackets to connect the rear of the dart solar to your roof rack system. And basically two plates. The two plates are'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for redundancy. We have two plates in the rear with four brackets. And for the front, we also have four brackets with two plates. I just remove the bolts so that I can visually show you how easily'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these basically move around the T- slot. Now, once you create pressure and clamp it, these brackets lock in place. So, there\'s no way that your dart solar can move. Now, one of the super exciting things about this design is that not'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only the dart solar can be installed in this direction, so the solar panels move to the front and the rear of the vehicle, but they can also be installed like this, side to side. So, you can install the dart solar so that the solar'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'panels expand like the doors of a vehicle. This gives you the added benefit. Not only do they cool your cabin, but they also provide shade to the side. If you\'re camping, for example. Now, if you have a pickup'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'truck, it\'s great because you can use the dart solar to cover the truck bed of your vehicle. You could put two or three if you have a long pickup truck, and you can also add one to the cabin. So, you could easily have 2,000 watts of solar'

[05:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'panel on top of a pickup truck in case you have to charge it or really power any other tools that you may have. We\'re now going to explain how the power of the solar panels can be transferred into'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your vehicle. So, all the solar panels connect to a solar combiner that we have engineered that lives on the T- slots of the spine of your Dart solar. We have created our own PCB board with the'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'diodes and electronics that your solar panels need and it\'s going to be sealed in a waterproof box. Basically, this box is going to have three inputs, one for every solar panel and one output. The'

[05:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar panels, we have custommade use XT60 connections which go in the in slots. In the out slot, we connect a custommade wire that we have engineered. It is very thin and very wide. It\'s'

[06:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about 1 mm thick or a little bit like uh 1/16th of an inch and it also uses an XT60 connection. So basically from the solar combiner that lives on the dart'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar itself and is waterproof, one cable comes out that is very thin and goes through the gasket of any door on your vehicle. It can go into your trunk, it can go through your window, through'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your door, wherever you have your preferred power unit. Now, if you live in a geography where there is extreme weather, we have also engineered a second cable that is a little bit thicker, but it\'s covered with an'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'insulator that can withstand up to -40 Celsius or -40 Fahrenheit. That way, your vehicle, even in extreme weathers, can safely transfer the power from the solar panels to wherever you place your'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'power unit on your vehicle. Now, the solar panels pass all their power to your power unit. And so, it\'s continually charging. Even when I\'m driving, even when I\'m stuck in traffic,'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s solar panels on the Dart solar facing the sun. So, as I\'m driving, I\'m charging the power unit. If I park in the shade or wherever I park, it doesn\'t matter. I connect my vehicle\'s charger to the power unit. And then, very'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easily, I can just pull it out and charge the vehicle. Now, one of the nice things about having a separate power unit and not have it integrated to the Dart Solar is you'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could use any power unit you want. If you go to the beach and it\'s already charged, you can take it out to the beach and play your speakers or a blender or whatever you want. So, one thing to note is you could use any power'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unit, but the voltage is important. We have tested the Dart Solar with the Echolow Delta Pro, the Delta 2, EG4, and other power units. But it\'s really important for you to know what voltage'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your power unit can accept. The solar combiner we have designed can output about 30 volts or about 90 volts. If you have a more premium power unit that can accept 90 or so volts of solar input,'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let us know when you put in an order so that we can include the correct solar combiner in your box. If you use, for example, a Echoflow Delta 2, which only accepts around 30 volts, then let us'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'know and we\'re going to ask you these questions when you place an order so that we send you the solar combiner that uses 30 volts. Now, one great thing about the Dart Solar is the customization and the adapters that'

[08:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'re going to build for it. For example, we ship the Dart Solar with a beautiful aluminum mill finish, and we don\'t anodize it or color it. We\'re going to release different adapters and modules that will allow you to color the'

