[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, a lot of people ask us about how much range can the Dark Solar add to their electric vehicle or how much power can it create. So, I\'m pretty bad with numbers and changing from imperial to'

[09:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'metrics. So, I\'ve written some notes. We engineered the Dart Solar in Los Angeles. So, in the summer in Los Angeles, this is the numbers that we\'re getting. And based on where you live, those numbers will fluctuate quite a'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little bit. You can search online about the different power creation rates for solar comparing Los Angeles to your city or country. when you take the 500 watt module, that\'s a 500 watt solar panel'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system when expanded, right? So, assuming you expand the dart solar from, let\'s say, 8 a.m. to 6 p.m., a good rule of thumb is to assume that you\'re only going to get 5 hours of really strong'

[09:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar. So, you take the 500 * 5, that\'s 2,500. The Dart solar module will be able to create around 2,000 watts uh 2,000 watt hours or 2500 watt hours per'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'day in a summer in Los Angeles. So if you have two modules, which typically two modules can fit on a sedan, you\'re going to get 1,000 watts of solar installed on your vehicle. That times 5,'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s about 5,000 W hours per day. Now, how does that translate to mileage or kilometers? A typical electric vehicle will use around 250 W hours per mile or let me see if I have the numbers here.'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '155 W hours per kilometer. So that translates to around 20 miles per day if you\'re using a 1,000 watt D solar system. So two modules or 32 kilometers'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'per day. Now that\'s in Los Angeles. Again, we we want to be very honest and transparent with you. It seems people exaggerate a lot of numbers. The solar'

[10:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'power of your vehicle and how much it can absorb really depends on where you live, but also how much it translates to miles or kilometers depends on your driving behavior. If you\'re driving, for example, a Tesla at 100 miles hour,'

[11:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is the way that we test the Dart Solar, we burn about 1,000 W hours per mile. That\'s four times more than the average use of a Tesla. So, your vehicle and your driving behavior really shifts'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the range. A good rule of thumb is just assume that you\'re going to get a good 5 hours of sunlight. So, a 500 watt module will create 2500 watts of power for you.'

[11:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A typical electric vehicle that\'s the standard out of the factory will probably go between 250 to 300 W hours per mile. Another thing to consider is'

[11:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the power unit that you use. If you use a power unit that has a pretty inexpensive or bad MPPPT, then it\'s going to create a lot of waste. Basically, what I\'m trying to say is when you order a Dart Solar, it really'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depends on where you live, your vehicle, your driving behavior, and the power unit that you use. Now, myself, when I use the Dart Solar on the vehicle, I get about 15 to 20 miles per day added to my'

[12:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'range. Now, this is great if you\'re going camping. This is great if you\'re only driving 20 hours per day. This is great if you have short commutes. But if you\'re planning to drive 600 miles, then'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Dart Solar is probably not the best product for you. However, if you\'re driving a short commute, let\'s say 20 miles per day, which is what the average American drives, it\'s a great product'

[12:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for you because you\'ll be able to capture about 20 miles of range per day as you park outside. And plus, the Dart Solar is going to keep your cabin cooler. Now that we have shown you what'

[12:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Dart solar product looks like and all the components, we\'re very excited to show you our new factory here in Los Angeles near Korea Town. It\'s 7,200 square ft and it has a tiny office area'

[12:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where our team has been diligently working. Now, as you can see, our R&amp;D section is in this area. There\'s a 4ft x4 ft CNC, 3D printers. There\'s a bending brake and a one ton crane that'

[13:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we can show you, a laser welding room within a shipping container. So, we\'re very excited to show you all the details and welcome to the factory. So, here we have the inventory section and we\'re going to take an opportunity to explain'

[13:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how we have designed this custommade solar panel. As you can see, it\'s very thin and it\'s backed by a layer of fiberglass on the rear and on the front it\'s covered by a layer of ETF or'

[13:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Teflon. It\'s very thin, but it basically protects the solar panel against any kind of friction or the elements. The entire solar panel is extremely flat. There is no junction box. There are no'

[13:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dodes. We just have bus bars here and the dodes go in the solar combiner that we have engineered. Now, to make sure that the solar panel does not fly off your vehicle, we\'ve created these flaps which are extremely strong. And these'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'flaps are what the solar panel uses to connect to the frame of the dart solar. Now, next to the solar panels, we have the inventory of the metals. And as you saw earlier in the video, this is one of'

[14:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the spines that comes in a Dart solar. All the metals are stored elsewhere, but we\'re now going to show you all the metal components that are part of the dark solar. So, the exciting part about the metals is that we\'re only using'

[14:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aluminum. This protects the metals against the elements much better and it also allows us to give this beautiful mill finish after we finish welding. We have the spine of the dart solar, which'

[14:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the T- slots that allow for the brackets to connect. We have crossarss in the front and they\'re all made out of 1/8 inch thick or 3 millimeter thick aluminum. There is no component that is'

[14:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'less thick than 3 mm to ensure that everything is extremely strong. We have the handlebars which you open. Inside of this spine, we have two pieces of metal'

[15:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that expand which create the telescopic arms which allow for the solar panels to increase in size 3x to about 15 ft wide. So, we have two telescopic arms, one for'

[15:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the left, one for the right, and then we have another set of crossarss and the handlebar. The rails have these holes for the polycarbonate to connect. And the polycarbonate has slots. So with'

[15:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thermal expansion, the polycarbonate can expand and contract without necessarily cracking. Now we\'re going to go to the welding room to show you how we put all of this together so it becomes one strong frame. So this welding area we'

[15:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'had to put inside of a 20ft shipping container because of lasers. We use a 200 W laser welder and the beads of the laser can bounce around. So, we have our'

[16:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'argon gas with a 200 W laser welder with a welding table that has a gigantic jig. We have covered all the metals that we use to put a frame together. But here is'

[16:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where it actually gets glued. We have this jig so that when the welding occurs, the metal cannot like deform because of the fusion. We keep everything pretty intact under pressure'

[16:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and we weld the top of the spine. the top of the side rail to the crossbar and then we flip it and we weld from inside how the crossbar connects to the side'

[16:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rail. That way when you look at the frame from the outside and you run your finger, it\'s super flush. Most of the welding occurs inside the frame. At the same time, the amount of wattage that we'

[16:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use in the laser welder does not go all the way through. So the telescopic arms that go into this hole, for example, will not be hindered by any type of metal expansion. And it\'s really'

[17:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exciting. The only bead lines are here on top of the frame, but that\'s covered by the static solar panel, which is always glued to the frame. So that there is a dynamic solar panel that moves'

[17:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forward, a dynamic solar panel that moves backward, and the static solar panel that also covers the entire frame. basically covers the bead lines and the other bead lines are from inside. So'

[17:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you look at an entire frame, it\'s seamless. And that\'s what I\'m going to show you right now. A frame with a static solar panel glued to it. So as I covered in the welding room, this is a frame of a dark solar already welded'

[17:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the static solar panel glued to it. As you can see, there are bead lines here on the bottom and on the inside of the corner. And then to cover the bead lines, we glue a static solar panel'

[17:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right on top of the frame. That way it looks very seamless, very clean. And this solar panel is exactly the same solar panel that we receive in our inventory. As you may remember, our'

[18:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar panels have a flap that bend. So we use this tool to basically go in, cut the flap, and create a static solar panel that perfectly fits onto the frame that we just welded. One of the unique'

[18:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'characteristics of the Dart Solar is the super thick protective shell that is aerodynamic that we have engineered on top of the module. So, we received the polycarbonate shells completely flat but'

[18:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pre-cut CNCed and it\'s really hard to bend this thing. It\'s about 4 mm thick and they typically use them in uh race car windshields or airplanes. So, what'

[18:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we have created is this gigantic jig. It\'s basically a bending brake with a one ton lifting capacity. We place the polycarbonate sheet in the brake. We'

[18:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lock it and clamp it down. And we slowly bend it to a very particular angle. Every time we bend it, we measure so that we make sure that the final polycarbonate is at a precise angle such'

[19:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that when we mount it onto the frame with the solar panels, you can see that the polycarbonate has a slight bend on top. And that\'s because of the precise angle that we can achieve with this jig'

[19:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that we have put together. Focusing on the R&amp;D is a great way to finish this video because it\'s because of the R&amp;D and the hard work of our engineers that we\'ve been able to prototype, test, and innovate so fast. So, thank you so much'

